import type {ResearchPost} from './fleet-data';

export const november07ResearchBatch:readonly ResearchPost[]=[{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-mixed-skill-queue-report-occupancy-without-hiding-idle-capacity","title":"How should a mixed-skill queue report occupancy without hiding idle capacity?","excerpt":"A measurement protocol for reporting occupancy across mixed-skill queues without letting a blended average hide idle capacity.","published":"2026-11-07","sourceDate":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-07","image":"/daily07/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-mixed-skill-queue-report-occupancy-without-hiding-idle-capacity.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks how a mixed-skill queue should report occupancy when agents handle more than one contact type. The population is one contact center site during a declared four-week window. The unit is a reporting interval, not an individual agent or a single call. Freeze the interval length and the skill definitions before looking at the results. Record the source of every input so a later analyst can reproduce the calculation. The question is not which team works hardest, but which queue has usable capacity."},{"heading":"Why blended occupancy misleads","body":"A blended occupancy figure divides total talk time by total paid time across every skill. That calculation can look healthy while one skill waits for an available agent and another skill sits underused. The average hides the queue a customer actually experiences. It also hides the moment when a cross-skilled agent is nominally available but assigned to the wrong queue. Report occupancy by skill and show the blend only as a secondary view. A blended number is useful for a site summary, never for a staffing decision on its own."},{"heading":"Define the unit and the clock","body":"State whether the interval is fifteen minutes, thirty minutes, or an hour. Use the same clock for offered contacts, handled contacts, and paid time. A measure built from a queue report that starts on the hour and a payroll file that starts on the half hour will never reconcile. Document the source and the extraction time for each input. Write down the time zone and the daylight-saving treatment. A one-hour shift in the clock can look like a capacity change when it is only a reporting artifact."},{"heading":"Separate productive and available time","body":"Productive time includes talk, hold, after-call work, and approved ancillary tasks. Available time is paid time when the agent could accept a contact but did not. Keep breaks, meetings, training, and system outages in separate categories. A single unproductive bucket makes it impossible to see whether idle capacity was usable or required. Split planned and unplanned events so the reader can tell a roster choice from an outage. The category definitions should be stable across the comparison period."},{"heading":"Allocate idle capacity by skill","body":"An agent who is available for billing and retention is not equally available to both queues at every moment. Allocate available time by the routing rules in force during the interval. If the rules change at noon, split the interval or document the change. A simple proportional split is acceptable only when the routing is genuinely proportional. Where the router gives priority to one skill, assign the available time to that skill and show the secondary capacity separately. The allocation rule belongs in the report, not in an analyst's notebook."},{"heading":"Handle cross-skilled agents","body":"Cross-skilled agents create a counting problem because their paid time can support more than one queue. Assign each interval to the skill the agent actually served, or use a documented allocation rule. Do not count the same available minute in two queues and then present the sum as a site total. The reconciliation table should show the allocation and the residual. A residual is acceptable when it is labeled and explained. An unexplained residual means the report is not yet ready for a capacity decision."},{"heading":"Set a reporting threshold","body":"Define a minimum volume below which a skill-level occupancy figure is not reported. A queue with three contacts in an interval can produce a dramatic percentage that says nothing useful. Use a threshold based on contacts and paid hours, and mark the excluded intervals rather than dropping them silently. The threshold should be set before the results are visible. Publish the excluded count beside the included count so the reader can see how much of the period was omitted. A suppressed interval is not a zero and should never be charted as one."},{"heading":"Build the reconciliation table","body":"The table should show paid hours, productive hours, available hours, handled contacts, and occupancy for each skill. Add a column for excluded time and one for the allocation rule. The site total should equal the sum of the skill rows after the allocation, and the difference should be explained rather than averaged away. Include a row for time that cannot be assigned to a skill. The table should tie to payroll and to the queue report on the same page. If the three sources disagree, show the bridge before showing the percentage."},{"heading":"Test with an adverse week","body":"Choose a week with a holiday, a system outage, or a training day. Recompute the occupancy and compare it with the original report. If the adverse week changes the ranking of the skills, the measure is not stable enough for a performance claim. Report both the normal and adverse results. Explain which category absorbed the unusual time and who approved the treatment. A measure that only works in a quiet week will mislead the first time the queue is stressed."},{"heading":"Independent replay","body":"Give a second analyst the same routing rules, source extracts, and definitions. Ask for an independent calculation without seeing the first result. Compare the skill totals and the allocation. Differences in the allocation rule should be resolved by the named operations owner, not by choosing the more flattering number. Record the resolution and update the definition sheet. The replay is evidence that the measure can survive a change of analyst, which is the first test of a durable operating metric."},{"heading":"Privacy and access","body":"Use aggregated interval data and avoid agent-level identifiers unless the review requires them. Restrict the working file to the analysts and the operations owner, and remove temporary access when the review closes. Report any field that was unavailable or redacted. Explain whether the redaction changes the ability to reconcile the total. Keep the extraction query with the report so the next review can use the same population. A measure that depends on a personal spreadsheet is difficult to audit and easy to misread."},{"heading":"Limits and next step","body":"The result depends on the routing rules, the interval length, the allocation method, and the quality of the source extracts. It does not prove that one skill is better managed than another. The next step is a written decision about which skill needs capacity, coaching, or a routing change. Name the owner and the date for the follow-up. Set a review point after the change so the team can see whether the capacity moved as intended. A measurement without a decision is only a longer report."},{"heading":"Decision the measure supports","body":"Use the skill-level occupancy to decide where to add hours, move cross-skilled agents, or change the routing priority. Do not use it to rank individual agents. A queue-level measure should lead to a queue-level decision, and the decision should name the owner and the date. Record the expected effect before the change so the next report can test it. Keep the blended figure for the executive summary but never for the staffing call. The measure earns trust when the decision and the result are visible together."},{"heading":"Coordinate with quality and training","body":"Occupancy is not the only reason a queue underperforms. A skill with low occupancy may still have long waits because the available agents lack the training to handle the contact. Share the skill-level view with quality and training before changing the roster. Ask whether the queue is short of people or short of people who can resolve the work. The answer may be a coaching plan rather than an extra shift. A capacity decision made without that conversation can add hours to a queue that still cannot answer the question."},{"heading":"Publish the monthly view","body":"Publish a one-page monthly view that shows occupancy by skill, the excluded intervals, and the allocation rule. Keep the format stable so the reader can compare one month with the next. Add a short note for any change in routing or skill definitions. The note is important because a change in the rule can move the percentage without any change in staffing. A monthly view that hides the rule change will be read as a performance change, and the argument will start from the wrong premise."},{"heading":"What would change the conclusion","body":"State the conditions that would change the recommendation. A new routing priority, a change in contact mix, or a sustained rise in repeat contacts could make the current allocation obsolete. Review those conditions at the next capacity meeting. A measure that is never revisited becomes a comfortable story about the past. The team should know which signals would cause it to reopen the decision, and those signals should be visible before the meeting begins."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers"},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g"},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","8% of the occupation works in business support services (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Report occupancy by skill before showing any blended site figure.","Allocate cross-skilled available time with a written rule and show the residual.","Suppress figures below a minimum volume instead of reporting unstable percentages.","Test the measure on a holiday or outage week before using it for a decision."],"faqs":[{"question":"Does this study rank agents?","answer":"No. It describes a measurement design and the decisions the resulting evidence can support."},{"question":"What counts as closure?","answer":"A reviewed result linked to the source, the definition, and the named owner who accepted it."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-can-first-contact-resolution-be-audited-when-tickets-are-merged","callcenteroutsourced-where-does-shrinkage-measurement-diverge-between-site-and-vendor"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue","body":"Bring one queue, its reporting extract, and the manager who owns the operating decision."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-can-first-contact-resolution-be-audited-when-tickets-are-merged","title":"Can first-contact resolution be audited when tickets are merged?","excerpt":"An audit design for first-contact resolution when ticket merges and reopened cases change the denominator.","published":"2026-11-07","sourceDate":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-07","image":"/daily07/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-can-first-contact-resolution-be-audited-when-tickets-are-merged.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks whether first-contact resolution can be audited when a customer's tickets are merged during or after the interaction. The population is customer cases that reached a closed state during a declared window. The unit is the customer's issue, not the ticket record. Freeze the window and the merge rule before extraction. Record the systems that hold the contact history and the case history. The audit is a test of the measurement design, not a performance ranking of individual agents."},{"heading":"What a merge changes","body":"A merge combines two or more ticket records into one visible case. The merge can reduce duplicate work, but it also changes the denominator. A customer who contacted the team twice may appear as one resolved case. An audit that counts tickets without counting merges will overstate resolution. The effect is largest in queues where the same customer writes, calls, and chats about one issue. Report the merge count beside the case count so the reader can see the shape of the population."},{"heading":"Define the contact and the case","body":"A contact is one interaction through one channel. A case is the customer's issue across contacts and channels. Record both identifiers and the relationship between them. The case key should survive a merge, a reassignment, and a reopen. If the key changes, the audit needs a crosswalk that preserves the history. The crosswalk should be generated from system records, not reconstructed by memory. A case that cannot be traced across systems is not a closed case for audit purposes."},{"heading":"Build the linkage rule","body":"Write the rule that decides when two records belong to the same case. Use verified identifiers such as a case number, an order number, or a confirmed customer reference. Do not merge on a similar name or a matching phone number alone. The rule should state who may merge, what evidence is required, and how a mistaken merge is reversed. Test the rule against a sample of known duplicates and known non-duplicates. A rule that cannot separate those groups will distort every rate built on top of it."},{"heading":"Classify the outcome","body":"Define resolved as the customer's stated need being met within the agreed scope, not as the ticket being closed. Separate resolved on first contact, resolved after follow-up, pending customer, pending internal, and unresolved. A closed ticket with no resolution evidence belongs in the unresolved category. Record the evidence that supports the classification, such as a confirmation, a completed action, or a customer reply. If the evidence is ambiguous, the case should be reviewed rather than counted as a success."},{"heading":"Sample merged and unmerged cases","body":"Draw a sample that includes merged cases, reopened cases, and cases with more than one channel. Compare the outcome classification with the customer's record and the agent's note. If merged cases are systematically more likely to be marked resolved, the merge process may be hiding repeat work. Stratify the sample by queue, channel, and case age. Report the result for each stratum before the overall figure. A single overall rate can conceal a problem that only appears in one part of the operation."},{"heading":"Test repeat contacts","body":"Measure repeat contacts within a defined period after closure. Use the customer identifier and the issue key, not the ticket number alone. A repeat contact about the same issue is evidence that the first resolution did not hold. Report the rate beside the first-contact resolution rate so the two numbers can be read together. Distinguish a genuine repeat from a related but new request. The reviewer should record why the second contact was treated as the same issue or a different one."},{"heading":"Measure at the customer, not the ticket","body":"Build the audit around the customer's issue. Count the contacts, the channels, and the elapsed time from the first contact to the final resolution. A case that took three contacts but ended with a correct answer is not a first-contact resolution, even if the final ticket looks clean. The customer's experience is the unit that matters for the service promise. Ticket-level metrics remain useful for workload, but they should not be presented as resolution evidence."},{"heading":"Report confidence and coverage","body":"Show the number of eligible cases, the number sampled, and the number excluded. Report the confidence interval or at least the sampling error for the headline rate. A precise-looking percentage built from a small or biased sample is not evidence of performance. State how merged, reopened, and multi-channel cases were handled. A reader should be able to tell whether the rate describes the whole population or only the easiest cases. Coverage is part of the result, not an appendix."},{"heading":"Independent replay","body":"Give a second reviewer the same cases, definitions, and merge log. Ask for an independent classification. Disagreements about whether a case was resolved should go to the named quality owner with the evidence attached. Do not resolve them by majority vote or by the seniority of the reviewer. Record the decision and update the example library. The replay tests whether the definition is strong enough to travel beyond the person who wrote it."},{"heading":"Privacy and access","body":"Use stable study keys and keep the identity crosswalk in the approved system. Restrict access to the fields needed for the audit and remove it at close. Report the fields that were unavailable, especially where a merge destroyed the original contact record. Explain whether the missing field affects the classification or only the description. Do not replace a missing contact with a plausible reconstruction. An audit that invents the history it is testing cannot support a resolution claim."},{"heading":"Limits and next step","body":"The result depends on the merge rule, the case key, the observation window, and the reviewer's judgment. It does not prove that an agent failed or that a customer was satisfied. The next step is to fix the merge rule or the follow-up process that the audit exposes. Name the owner and the date for the fix. Re-run the audit on the same population definition after the change so the comparison is meaningful. A second audit with a different definition is a new baseline, not proof of improvement."},{"heading":"Decision the audit supports","body":"Use the result to decide whether the queue needs a better case-linking rule, a follow-up owner, or a different definition of resolution. Name the decision owner and the date. Keep the ticket-level and customer-level views separate so neither one is mistaken for the other. Publish the definition beside the rate so the client can challenge the method. A resolution measure is only useful when the people who own the work can see how it was built."},{"heading":"Account for reopenings","body":"A reopened case is not a new case, but it is also not a first-contact resolution. Record the reopen reason, the time since closure, and the owner who reopened it. Report reopened cases separately and show how they affect the headline rate. A queue that closes cases quickly but sees many reopens may look better on the first metric than it deserves. The reopen count is the correction that keeps the resolution claim honest."},{"heading":"Use a control chart for stability","body":"Plot the first-contact resolution rate over time with the sample size. Look for a shift or a trend that is larger than the normal variation. A single month above or below the average is not evidence of a lasting change. Use the chart to decide when to investigate and when to wait for more data. The chart should show the merge count and the reopen count on the same page so the reader can see which input moved."},{"heading":"Document the linkage changes","body":"Every change to the merge rule or the case key should have a date, an owner, and a reason. Keep the old rule with the historical data so a prior rate can still be interpreted. If the change is material, report a bridge between the old and new definitions. A rate that moves after a linkage change may describe the rule, not the service. Documenting the change is the difference between an audit and a before-and-after comparison with no control."},{"heading":"Publish the definition with the number","body":"Put the resolution definition, the observation window, and the merge rule beside every published rate. A reader who cannot see the definition will assume the most familiar one and draw a different conclusion. Include the eligible count and the sample count in the same block. Keep the wording stable between reports. If the wording changes, treat the report as a new series and say so. A number without its definition is an invitation to argue about the wrong question."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers"},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g"},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","5% projected decline in employment, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings from replacement needs (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Count the customer's issue, not the surviving ticket, as the unit of analysis.","Preserve a case key across merges, reopens, and channel changes.","Report repeat contacts beside first-contact resolution so the two numbers explain each other.","Send unresolved classification disagreements to a named quality owner."],"faqs":[{"question":"Does this study rank agents?","answer":"No. It describes a measurement design and the decisions the resulting evidence can support."},{"question":"What counts as closure?","answer":"A reviewed result linked to the source, the definition, and the named owner who accepted it."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-where-does-shrinkage-measurement-diverge-between-site-and-vendor","callcenteroutsourced-what-breaks-when-a-forecast-borrows-another-industry-s-seasonality-curve"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue","body":"Bring one queue, its reporting extract, and the manager who owns the operating decision."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-where-does-shrinkage-measurement-diverge-between-site-and-vendor","title":"Where does shrinkage measurement diverge between site and vendor?","excerpt":"A reconciliation method for site and vendor shrinkage reports, with shared categories, clocks, and a published bridge.","published":"2026-11-07","sourceDate":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-07","image":"/daily07/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-where-does-shrinkage-measurement-diverge-between-site-and-vendor.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief examines why two reports of the same call center's shrinkage can disagree. The population is one site's scheduled agent hours during a declared four-week window. The unit is a paid hour and its assigned category. Freeze the categories and the payroll source before comparing the site and vendor reports. Record the report version and the extraction time for both sides. The goal is a shared explanation of the hours, not a winner in an argument about percentages."},{"heading":"Define shrinkage categories","body":"Shrinkage usually includes time paid but not available for contacts. The category list may include breaks, meals, meetings, training, coaching, system downtime, unplanned absence, and special projects. Write each definition with an inclusion and an exclusion example. A category named other is not a definition. Test the list against a week of real records before publishing it. If the same record can fit two categories, the definitions are not yet ready for a comparison."},{"heading":"Who counts as unavailable","body":"An agent in a team meeting is unavailable for contacts but may still be productive for the business. An agent waiting for a system to recover is unavailable through no fault of the team. Decide whether the report measures contact availability, schedule adherence, or paid unavailability. The three questions produce different numbers. Label the report with the question it answers. A reader who assumes the wrong question will draw the wrong conclusion even when every number is correct."},{"heading":"Paid break versus unproductive time","body":"A short rest period is generally counted as hours worked under federal call center guidance, while a bona fide meal period may be unpaid when the employee is relieved of duty. The payroll treatment and the workforce report must use the same rule. A report that treats a paid break as shrinkage and a payroll file that treats it as productive time will never reconcile. Document the rule in both systems and test it on a sample of records. The legal treatment and the operational category are related, but they are not the same field."},{"heading":"Schedule changes and adherence","body":"A schedule change made after the roster is published can look like shrinkage in one report and adherence in another. Record the change, the approver, and the effective time. Compare the actual interval with the schedule in force at that moment, not the schedule that was published at the start of the month. Keep the change log with the reconciliation. A last-minute change that moves an agent from a contact queue to a training room should appear as training, not as unexplained absence."},{"heading":"Site versus vendor clock","body":"The site may measure from the badge-in time, while the vendor measures from the first login. The difference includes the time an agent spends walking to the desk and starting the system. That time may be compensable work. State which clock each report uses and reconcile the gap explicitly. Test the difference at the start and end of a shift, not only at the middle. A clock difference that looks small per agent can become a material number across a large floor."},{"heading":"Reconcile the denominator","body":"Start with total paid hours from payroll. Subtract the categories that both reports agree are unavailable. Compare the remaining available hours with the queue's logged time. Any difference should be assigned to a named category or reported as unexplained. A residual that is simply labeled variance is not a reconciliation. Set a tolerance for the unexplained amount and escalate when it is exceeded. The tolerance should be agreed before the first comparison, not after the numbers arrive."},{"heading":"Test a high-shrinkage week","body":"Choose a week with a holiday, a training day, or a system outage. Recompute both reports and compare the category totals. If the difference grows in the adverse week, the reports may be treating the event differently. Document the treatment before drawing a conclusion about performance. Show the event in the bridge as its own line. A holiday that is coded as absence on one side and holiday pay on the other should be visible, not buried in a total."},{"heading":"Report the bridge, not just the total","body":"Publish a bridge that shows how the site total becomes the vendor total. List each adjustment with its source and owner. A single percentage for shrinkage is not enough to explain a gap between two organizations. The bridge is the evidence that the two reports describe the same hours. Keep the bridge format stable across months so the reader can compare one period with the next. A new format every month makes the trend impossible to follow."},{"heading":"Independent replay","body":"Give a second analyst the payroll extract, the roster, the queue log, and the category definitions. Ask for an independent bridge. Compare the adjustments and the residual. Differences in policy should go to the named operations and finance owners, not to the analyst who produced the more convenient number. Record the resolution and update the definition sheet. The replay should be repeatable by someone who did not attend the original meeting."},{"heading":"Privacy and access","body":"Use aggregated hours where possible and keep agent-level records in the approved system. Restrict the review to the minimum fields needed and remove temporary access at close. Report any payroll field that was unavailable or could not be matched to the roster. Explain whether the gap changes a category total or only an individual record. Do not substitute a team average for a missing individual value unless the method is documented and accepted."},{"heading":"Limits and next step","body":"The result depends on the payroll cut-off, the schedule version, the category definitions, and the quality of the queue log. It does not prove that one team is working harder. The next step is a shared definition sheet and a monthly bridge owned by both sides. Name the owners and the date for the first joint review. A bridge that is produced by only one side is a report, not a reconciliation."},{"heading":"Decision the measure supports","body":"Use the reconciled shrinkage to decide where to add coverage, fix a schedule change process, or correct a payroll coding rule. Do not use it to rank individual agents. A gap between site and vendor is a process question before it is a performance question. Record the expected effect of the fix and review it in the next bridge. The measure is working when both sides can explain the same hours without renegotiating the definitions."},{"heading":"Coordinate with payroll","body":"Payroll owns the hours, and workforce planning owns the category. The two teams should agree on the cut-off, the rounding rule, and the treatment of a shift that crosses midnight. A shift that starts on Sunday and ends on Monday can appear in different weeks if the rule is not written. Test the rule on one complex pay period. The reconciliation should be signed by both owners before it is used in a client report."},{"heading":"Set the monthly review agenda","body":"Use the same agenda each month: paid hours, category changes, unexplained residual, and the one action that will reduce the gap. Keep the meeting short and evidence-led. Invite the operations owner who can change the schedule and the finance owner who can change the coding. A review without those two people produces observations but not corrections. The agenda should end with a named owner and a due date for each adjustment."},{"heading":"What would change the conclusion","body":"A new payroll system, a change in the shift structure, or a new client requirement can change the bridge. State those triggers and review them at the start of each quarter. If a trigger occurs, rebuild the bridge from the source rather than adjusting the old one. A bridge that is patched without a new source check becomes a story about last year's process. The team should know when to start fresh and when to keep the current comparison."},{"heading":"Keep the bridge reproducible","body":"Store the payroll extract, the roster version, the queue log, and the category sheet with the finished bridge. Name the person who produced each input and the time it was pulled. A reviewer should be able to rebuild the same totals without asking for a meeting. If an input cannot be retrieved, mark the bridge as partial. A reconciliation that depends on a file someone kept on a laptop is not repeatable, and it will not survive a change of analyst."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers"},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g"},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"keyStats":["$21.53 median hourly wage for customer service representatives, May 2025 (BLS)","2 to 4 weeks of typical short-term on-the-job training (BLS)","8% of the occupation works in business support services (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Start the reconciliation with total paid hours from payroll.","Use the same paid-break and meal-period rule in payroll and workforce reports.","Publish a bridge from the site total to the vendor total with every adjustment named.","Test the bridge in a holiday or outage week before accepting the annual figure."],"faqs":[{"question":"Does this study rank agents?","answer":"No. It describes a measurement design and the decisions the resulting evidence can support."},{"question":"What counts as closure?","answer":"A reviewed result linked to the source, the definition, and the named owner who accepted it."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-breaks-when-a-forecast-borrows-another-industry-s-seasonality-curve","callcenteroutsourced-what-makes-a-quality-scorecard-portable-across-two-vendor-sites"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue","body":"Bring one queue, its reporting extract, and the manager who owns the operating decision."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-what-breaks-when-a-forecast-borrows-another-industry-s-seasonality-curve","title":"What breaks when a forecast borrows another industry's seasonality curve?","excerpt":"A backtest plan for borrowed seasonality curves, including holiday calendars, contact mix, and interval-level error.","published":"2026-11-07","sourceDate":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-07","image":"/daily07/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-what-breaks-when-a-forecast-borrows-another-industry-s-seasonality-curve.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks what happens when a call center forecast borrows a seasonality curve from another industry. The population is one queue's contact volume during a declared historical window. The unit is a forecast interval and its error. Freeze the interval, the horizon, and the error measure before testing the curve. Record the history available and the date of the last complete period. The test is whether the borrowed curve improves a staffing decision, not whether it looks plausible in a chart."},{"heading":"What a borrowed curve assumes","body":"A borrowed curve assumes that the other industry's weekly shape, holiday effects, and seasonal peaks apply to this queue. That assumption may hold for a shared retail calendar and fail for a business with a different billing cycle. The curve is a hypothesis, not a neutral input. Write the assumptions down before fitting anything. A curve that cannot state its assumptions cannot be tested, because every miss becomes an exception after the fact."},{"heading":"Check the demand unit","body":"Define whether the forecast is contacts, calls, tickets, or customer issues. A curve built from call volume may not fit a queue that receives most of its work through chat and email. Even within calls, a transferred contact and a new contact are different units. Use the same unit in the history and the forecast. A growth in transfers can look like demand growth when the customer issue count is flat. The unit should match the resource the forecast is meant to staff."},{"heading":"Compare holiday calendars","body":"List the holidays that change the queue's volume and the days the business closes. Compare the borrowed curve's holiday assumptions with the local calendar. A curve that treats a local holiday as a normal weekday will overstaff the queue and misread the following day's recovery. Mark the holiday and the recovery day separately. The day after a closure often carries displaced demand, and a borrowed curve may have no equivalent event."},{"heading":"Test the curve against local history","body":"Fit the borrowed curve to the local history and compare it with a simple local baseline. Use at least one full seasonal cycle if the data exists. If the borrowed curve does not beat the baseline on a held-out period, it is adding complexity without evidence. Keep the baseline simple enough to explain to an operations manager. A model that wins by a small margin but cannot be understood is a poor choice for a live staffing process."},{"heading":"Separate trend from season","body":"A curve can fit the seasonal shape while missing a long-term change in volume. Detrend the history before comparing the seasonal pattern, then add the local trend back for the forecast. A queue that is growing or shrinking will otherwise attribute the trend to the season. Plot the trend and the seasonal index separately. The reviewer should see whether the borrowed shape survives after the trend is removed."},{"heading":"Model the contact mix","body":"Forecast the total and the mix. A queue with a stable total but a changing mix can still miss its staffing plan, because different contact types have different handling times. Test whether the borrowed curve improves the total, the mix, or both. Build a separate error measure for each contact type. A curve that improves the total while making the complex queue worse is not an improvement for the customer who needs the complex queue."},{"heading":"Run a backtest","body":"Hold out the most recent period and forecast it with the borrowed curve and with the local baseline. Compare mean absolute percentage error and the bias. Report the error by interval and by day of week, not only as one annual number. A curve that performs well on average can fail on the days that matter most. Include at least one holiday and one ordinary week in the test. The backtest should resemble the decisions the team will actually make."},{"heading":"Report error by interval","body":"Show the forecast error for each interval and the staffing consequence. A ten percent error at a low-volume hour may be manageable, while the same error at peak may create a long wait. Translate the error into positions and wait time so the operations owner can judge the risk. Mark the intervals where the forecast changes the staffing plan. An error that does not change a decision is less urgent than one that does, even when the percentage looks similar."},{"heading":"Independent replay","body":"Give a second analyst the same history, the same curve, and the same backtest rules. Ask for an independent result. Compare the forecast error and the treatment of holidays. Differences in the data cut-off or the holiday calendar should be resolved before the forecast is used for staffing. Record the resolution and the final parameters. The replay tests the forecast process, not the analyst's loyalty to the borrowed curve."},{"heading":"Limits and next step","body":"The result depends on the history available, the stability of the queue, and the chosen error measure. It does not prove that the borrowed curve is always wrong. The next step is to keep the local baseline, document the borrowed assumptions, and revisit the choice after the next seasonal peak. Name the review date and the owner. A forecast choice that is never revisited becomes an unexplained tradition."},{"heading":"Decision the forecast supports","body":"Use the backtest to decide whether to adopt the borrowed curve, blend it with local history, or keep the local model. Name the owner of the forecast and the date of the next review. A forecast is a decision aid, and its assumptions should be visible to the people who staff against it. Record the staffing change that the forecast is expected to produce. The test is complete when the team can explain why the chosen curve is better than the simpler alternative."},{"heading":"Check model stability","body":"Re-estimate the model on a rolling window and watch whether the seasonal factors move. A stable curve is easier to defend than one that changes shape every month. If the factors move sharply, look for a change in the business before blaming the model. Record the estimation window and the last update. A forecast that is never refreshed will slowly become a description of an older queue, even when the code still runs without error."},{"heading":"Involve the frontline","body":"Ask the supervisors and agents whether the forecast matches what they see. They know about local events, school calendars, and service outages that may not be in the data. Treat their observations as hypotheses to test, not as proof. A short weekly note can explain an unusual interval before it becomes a mystery in the monthly review. The forecast improves when the people who staff it can challenge its assumptions."},{"heading":"Document the chosen model","body":"Write a short model note with the data window, the chosen curve, the holiday treatment, the error measure, and the next review date. Keep the note beside the forecast file so a new analyst can reproduce the result. Include the baseline and the reason it was rejected. A model that cannot be reconstructed is a personal artifact, not an operating tool. The note should be short enough to read before the staffing meeting."},{"heading":"What would change the conclusion","body":"A new product launch, a change in the billing cycle, or a shift in channel mix can invalidate the borrowed curve. State those triggers and revisit the model when one occurs. Do not wait for the annual review if the business has already changed. A forecast is only as good as the stability of the process it describes, and the team should know when that stability has ended."},{"heading":"Review with staffing","body":"Walk the forecast through the staffing plan before publishing it. Ask whether the interval-level error changes the number of positions or the start time of a shift. A forecast that is statistically better but operationally unusable is not an improvement. Record the staffing decision that depends on the forecast and the margin the manager uses. The review should include the supervisor who will have to explain the plan to the floor."},{"heading":"Publish the forecast note","body":"Publish a short note with the chosen curve, the baseline, the error by interval, and the next review date. Keep it beside the forecast file, not in a separate folder that no one opens. The note should be understandable to a supervisor who does not build models. Include one sentence on what would cause the team to change the curve. A forecast that cannot be explained to the floor will be ignored when the queue gets busy."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers"},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g"},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","5% projected decline in employment, 2025 to 2035 (BLS)","289,500 projected annual openings, mostly replacement (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Test a borrowed curve against a local baseline on a held-out period.","Compare holiday calendars and contact units before fitting the curve.","Report forecast error by interval and by day, not only as one annual average.","Translate error into positions and wait time for the staffing decision."],"faqs":[{"question":"Does this study rank agents?","answer":"No. It describes a measurement design and the decisions the resulting evidence can support."},{"question":"What counts as closure?","answer":"A reviewed result linked to the source, the definition, and the named owner who accepted it."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-what-makes-a-quality-scorecard-portable-across-two-vendor-sites","callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-mixed-skill-queue-report-occupancy-without-hiding-idle-capacity"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue","body":"Bring one queue, its reporting extract, and the manager who owns the operating decision."}},{"slug":"callcenteroutsourced-what-makes-a-quality-scorecard-portable-across-two-vendor-sites","title":"What makes a quality scorecard portable across two vendor sites?","excerpt":"A calibration protocol for making a quality scorecard comparable across two vendor sites and defensible under review.","published":"2026-11-07","sourceDate":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-07","image":"/daily07/callcenteroutsourced/callcenteroutsourced-what-makes-a-quality-scorecard-portable-across-two-vendor-sites.webp","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":"This brief asks what makes a quality scorecard portable across two vendor sites that serve the same client. The population is scored customer interactions during a declared calibration window. The unit is one interaction and its scored behaviors. Freeze the behavior definitions and the sampling rule before comparing sites. Record the reviewers, the recording set, and the scoring date. The question is whether the instrument travels, not whether one site has nicer facilities or a more familiar accent."},{"heading":"What portability means","body":"A portable scorecard produces comparable scores when two trained reviewers apply it to similar interactions. Portability does not require identical customers or identical products. It requires the same behavior definitions, the same evidence standard, and a calibration process that finds disagreement before it affects a performance decision. A score that depends on the reviewer's location is not a measure of the agent. Write the standard down so a new reviewer can apply it without asking the original author."},{"heading":"Fix the behavior definitions","body":"Write each behavior as an observable action with an inclusion and an exclusion. Replace vague labels such as empathy or ownership with specific evidence, such as acknowledging the customer's stated impact and naming the next step. A definition that requires the reviewer to infer intent will drift across sites. Give each behavior a short example and a counterexample. The example library is part of the instrument, not a training nicety that can be skipped when the queue is busy."},{"heading":"Calibrate reviewers","body":"Have reviewers from both sites score the same recorded interactions independently. Compare the scores behavior by behavior, not only the total. Discuss every disagreement and update the definition or the example library. Record the calibration date, the participants, and the resolution. Keep the scored sample so a later reviewer can repeat the exercise. A calibration that produces only a total score hides the definition problem that created the gap."},{"heading":"Test with recorded interactions","body":"Use a sample that includes difficult calls, silent customers, escalated calls, and calls with system failures. A scorecard that works only on clean calls is not portable. Include interactions from both sites and from more than one product or queue. Balance the sample so one site's easy calls do not set the standard for the other. Report the sample composition beside the agreement rate. The reader should know which kinds of interactions the score describes."},{"heading":"Separate coaching from scoring","body":"A scorecard used for coaching can tolerate more context and discussion than one used for a formal performance decision. State the purpose before the calibration. If the same score serves both purposes, define how a coaching note is kept separate from the scored evidence. Do not let a coaching conversation change a score after the review. The agent should be able to see the evidence and the definition that produced the result. A score that changes without a recorded reason cannot support a fair conversation."},{"heading":"Handle disagreement","body":"Define what happens when reviewers cannot agree. The case should go to a named calibration owner with the evidence and the competing definitions. The owner decides, records the reason, and updates the example library. A majority vote among reviewers is not a substitute for an owned definition. Track the cases that required escalation and the behaviors they involved. A recurring disagreement is a signal that the definition is incomplete, not that the reviewers are careless."},{"heading":"Recheck after drift","body":"Calibrate again after a policy change, a product launch, or a change in the reviewer team. Drift is normal, and an annual calibration may be too slow for a fast-changing queue. Use a short monthly sample and a deeper quarterly review. Compare the new agreement rate with the prior rate. If agreement falls after a change, pause the site comparison until the definition and the training are aligned again. A score produced during a known drift should be labeled as provisional."},{"heading":"Report coverage and confidence","body":"Show how many interactions were eligible, sampled, and scored. Report the agreement rate and the behaviors with the most disagreement. A site score without coverage and agreement data is difficult to defend when an agent or a client challenges it. Include the sampling method and the period. If the sample is small, say so instead of reporting a precise decimal. The reader needs enough information to judge whether the comparison is stable."},{"heading":"Independent replay","body":"Give a third reviewer the same sample and definitions without seeing the site scores. Compare the third review with each site's result. If the third review aligns with one site more than the other, the scorecard may be measuring reviewer style rather than agent behavior. Record the comparison and the definition changes. The replay is the strongest evidence that the scorecard can travel beyond the two original teams."},{"heading":"Privacy and access","body":"Use recorded interactions only where the customer consent and the client policy allow it. Restrict access to the reviewers and the calibration owner, and remove temporary access at close. Report any interaction that could not be used because the recording or consent was missing. Explain whether the missing interaction changes the sample balance. Do not replace a missing recording with a reconstructed summary unless the method is approved and disclosed."},{"heading":"Limits and next step","body":"The result depends on the sample, the definitions, the reviewer training, and the calibration process. It does not prove that one site delivers better service. The next step is to publish the agreed definitions and run the calibration on a fixed schedule. Name the calibration owner and the review date. Keep the old definitions with their effective dates so a historical score can still be interpreted after a change."},{"heading":"Decision the scorecard supports","body":"Use the calibrated score to decide where coaching, definition work, or a process fix is needed. Do not use an uncalibrated total to rank sites or agents. A portable scorecard is a shared measuring instrument, and the instrument must be checked before the measurements are trusted. Record the decision and the expected change. The next calibration should show whether the instrument became more consistent, not only whether the score moved."},{"heading":"Build the example library","body":"Collect short, approved examples for each behavior and each common disagreement. Store them where both sites can reach them and label them with the definition version. The library should include a clean example, a borderline example, and a clear miss. Review the examples when the definition changes. An example library that is never updated becomes a source of drift, because reviewers keep applying the old interpretation to the new wording."},{"heading":"Train new reviewers","body":"Give every new reviewer the same introduction to the definitions, the evidence standard, and the calibration process. Have them score a known set before they review live work. Compare their scores with the calibration owner and discuss the gaps. Do not put a new reviewer on a site comparison until the known set is completed. Training that consists only of reading the scorecard will not produce a consistent reviewer."},{"heading":"Audit the auditor","body":"Sample the reviewers as well as the agents. A reviewer whose scores drift from the calibration set may need coaching or a definition refresher. Keep the reviewer sample separate from the agent performance process. The purpose is to protect the instrument, not to punish the reviewer. Report the reviewer agreement rate to the calibration owner and act on it before the next site comparison."},{"heading":"What would change the conclusion","body":"A new client requirement, a product change, or a change in the recording consent rule can make the current scorecard obsolete. State those triggers and revisit the definitions when one occurs. Do not compare a new score with an old one unless the bridge is documented. The scorecard should have a visible version and a review date so the reader knows which instrument produced the number."},{"heading":"Keep the calibration record","body":"Store the sample, the independent scores, the disagreements, and the final decisions in one place. Name the calibration owner and the date. A later reviewer should be able to see why a definition changed without reading a long email thread. Summarize the top three disagreements in the record. A calibration record that is complete but unreadable will not prevent the next reviewer from repeating the same disagreement."}],"sources":[{"name":"Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","url":"https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},{"name":"Fact Sheet #64: Call Centers under the Fair Labor Standards Act","url":"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/64-flsa-call-centers"},{"name":"GAO-20-283G: Assessing Data Reliability","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-20-283g"},{"name":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0","url":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"keyStats":["2.67 million U.S. customer service representative jobs in 2025 (BLS)","$21.53 median hourly wage in May 2025 (BLS)","2 to 4 weeks of typical short-term training (BLS)"],"takeaways":["Write observable behavior definitions with inclusion and exclusion examples.","Calibrate reviewers on the same recorded interactions before comparing sites.","Report agreement, coverage, and sample size beside every site score.","Recalibrate after policy, product, or reviewer-team changes."],"faqs":[{"question":"Does this study rank agents?","answer":"No. It describes a measurement design and the decisions the resulting evidence can support."},{"question":"What counts as closure?","answer":"A reviewed result linked to the source, the definition, and the named owner who accepted it."}],"related":["callcenteroutsourced-how-should-a-mixed-skill-queue-report-occupancy-without-hiding-idle-capacity","callcenteroutsourced-can-first-contact-resolution-be-audited-when-tickets-are-merged"],"internalLinks":["/research","/services","/contact-us"],"serviceHandoff":{"href":"/contact-us","label":"Review the queue","body":"Bring one queue, its reporting extract, and the manager who owns the operating decision."}}];
